 \section{Motivation}
 \label{sec:intro-motivation}
 
-Increasing the switching frequency reduces the energy transferred per switching period and can allow smaller passive components. It also leaves less time for commutation, while winding loss, switching loss, and parasitic impedance become more important. A smaller magnetic component is useful only if the power stage, layout, gate drive, and controller can operate with it.
+MHz switching permits reduced passive-component size but increases sensitivity to commutation time, winding loss, and parasitic impedance. Magnetic integration, gate timing, layout, and control must therefore be evaluated at the installed operating point.
 
 Resonant conversion and GaN devices make MHz operation practical. Resonant current can move the half-bridge node during dead time and reduce turn-on loss, while fast GaN devices allow short switching intervals. A charge-pump PFC rectifier and an integrated resonant LED driver operating near \SI{1}{\mega\hertz} were demonstrated in earlier DTU work \cite{ammar2020chargepump,ammar2021led}. In these converters, capacitor charge transfer shapes the input-current envelope without a separate boost-PFC current controller.
 
-The LED load used in the reference design and a USB-C load place different demands on the converter. A USB-C load can be absent at startup, connect after the secondary has charged, or disconnect while energy remains in the resonant tank. Different output-voltage settings also change the demand seen by the resonant stage. Stored secondary energy and average output power therefore both had to be controlled.
+USB-C operation introduces startup without a load, delayed attachment, load removal, and multiple output-voltage settings. These conditions alter the demand on the resonant stage and require control of both secondary stored energy and average transferred power.
 
 The supplied planar inductor and transformer set the starting point. Their PCB windings provide a low profile. The board had to accommodate their footprint, winding connections, insulation spacing, and mounting, while the resonant operating point had to match their electrical parameters.
 
