 \section*{Abstract}
 \addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Abstract}
 
-A USB-C laboratory power supply based on an earlier DTU charge-pump power-factor-correction (PFC) resonant converter was built and tested using the supplied planar magnetics. The controller was developed to handle a detachable load. Secondary voltage had to be regulated under load, while further charging had to be limited after disconnection because energy remained in the resonant tank and capacitors.
+A digitally controlled USB-C laboratory supply was implemented using an inherited charge-pump power-factor-correction (PFC) resonant topology and planar magnetics. Burst control was developed for secondary-bulk regulation, startup sequencing, and energy limitation at no load and after load removal.
 
-The switching frequency was kept fixed at a nominal \SI{1}{\mega\hertz}, and energy transfer was adjusted by changing the duration of gallium-nitride (GaN) half-bridge bursts with the microcontroller (MCU). Voltage feedback was converted into digital readings and transferred automatically to memory using direct memory access (DMA), avoiding repeated processor waits for individual measurements. The isolated secondary bulk bus was regulated using the voltage-control firmware. Limited precharge was applied until feedback became available. Separate duty limits were used under load and at no load. The tested \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} USB-C settings are provided by a downstream output stage.
+The nominal \SI{1}{\mega\hertz} carrier was retained, while average transfer was regulated through gallium-nitride (GaN) half-bridge burst enable. Circular direct memory access (DMA) provided nonblocking feedback acquisition. Startup precharge and separate loaded/no-load duty limits were implemented. A downstream output stage provided the tested \SI{5}{\volt}, \SI{9}{\volt}, \SI{15}{\volt}, and \SI{20}{\volt} USB-C settings.
 
-With an AC input recorded as \SI{240}{\volt}, \SI{50}{\hertz}, matched voltage and current readings at the \SI{20}{\volt} setting gave output powers of \SIrange{73.97}{79.89}{\watt} at the load. Average output voltage was \SIrange{19.973}{19.991}{\volt}, with recorded peak-to-peak ripple of \SIrange{0.416}{0.452}{\volt}. Logged power factor was 0.97--0.99 and current total harmonic distortion was \SIrange{2.23}{3.98}{\percent}. An initial efficiency estimate of \SIrange{76.5}{77.1}{\percent} was calculated from the paired input and output measurements. Uncertainty in their timing and calibration limits the accuracy of this estimate. Controller operation was recorded during the tested no-load and load-removal events. A thermal image labeled \SI{20}{\volt}, \SI{4}{\ampere} showed a maximum surface temperature of \SI{90.4}{\celsius}. Further loss measurements, improved heat removal, and protection-timing tests are still needed.
+At \SI{240}{\volt}, \SI{50}{\hertz} input, matched measurements at the \SI{20}{\volt} setting yielded \SIrange{73.97}{79.89}{\watt} at the load. Average output voltage was \SIrange{19.973}{19.991}{\volt}, with \SIrange{0.416}{0.452}{\volt} recorded peak-to-peak ripple. Logged power factor was 0.97--0.99 and current total harmonic distortion was \SIrange{2.23}{3.98}{\percent}. Paired input/output readings gave preliminary efficiency estimates of \SIrange{76.5}{77.1}{\percent}, limited by acquisition timing and calibration uncertainty. No-load and load-removal operation were recorded. A thermal image labeled \SI{20}{\volt}, \SI{4}{\ampere} showed a \SI{90.4}{\celsius} surface maximum, motivating further loss and thermal characterization.
 
-Development continued with a V2 board integrating the controller, GaN stage, isolated feedback, and USB-C circuit on a four-layer PCB. Lower half-bridge heating was observed after the tank and timing changes, but input-bridge heating remained unresolved (\cref{sec:res-v2-testing}). Bench performance was broadly comparable to the first prototype. Secondary-winding loss and a restricted input current path were suspected limitations, but neither was isolated quantitatively.
+V2 integrated the MCU, GaN stage, isolated feedback, and USB-C circuit on a four-layer PCB. Lower half-bridge heating was observed after tank and timing changes, while input-bridge heating remained unresolved (\cref{sec:res-v2-testing}). Bench performance remained comparable to the first prototype. Suspected winding and input-path limitations were not isolated quantitatively. The V3 edge-card layout retained secondary rectification and omitted the USB-PD output section, giving an estimated net volume reduction of approximately \SI{8}{\percent}. Replacement planar magnetics were not installed. A complete-system density comparison must include the external regulated USB-PD stage.
 
-A slimmer V3 edge-card design was prepared without the USB-PD output section, leaving secondary rectification. Its estimated net volume reduction was about \SI{8}{\percent}. New planar magnetics were not installed within the available time, so higher sustained power was not demonstrated. A complete USB-C supply would also require an external regulated USB-PD stage.
-
-Digitally controlled operation at approximately \SI{80}{\watt} was demonstrated. In the revised plan, a \SI{100}{\watt} continuous target and a \SI{65}{\watt} continuous minimum are retained. Before either can be claimed as a continuous rating, thermal and stability tests must be run for a defined duration. Full-range zero-voltage switching, USB-PD conformance, safety, and EMC also need dedicated verification. The next work is to resolve the magnetic and current-path limits, then evaluate the complete V3 arrangement. Operation toward \SI{240}{\watt} USB-PD Extended Power Range (EPR) remains a longer-term goal.
+Digitally controlled operation near \SI{80}{\watt} was demonstrated. The \SI{100}{\watt} continuous target and \SI{65}{\watt} continuous minimum require defined-duration thermal and stability validation. Full-range zero-voltage switching, USB-PD conformance, safety, and EMC remain unverified. Operation toward \SI{240}{\watt} USB-PD Extended Power Range (EPR) is a longer-term development objective.
